WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.897
350 metres below this drilling rig, one
steel pipe is holding everything up.

2
00:00:06.937 --> 00:00:10.834
Every wave that lifts the
rig tugs on the riser.

3
00:00:11.214 --> 00:00:13.280
The riser swings the blowout preventer,

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.427
and the blowout preventer
bends the wellhead.

5
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.457
A little to one side.

6
00:00:18.497 --> 00:00:20.744
Then a little to the other.

7
00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:24.039
About 7.5 million times a year.

8
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:28.613
No single one of those
cycles is dangerous.

9
00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:34.163
But together they can grow a crack in
a weld that nobody will ever inspect:

10
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:40.921
in the conductor, just below the template,
or in the template slot that holds it.

11
00:00:40.961 --> 00:00:45.455
So the question every well
engineer has to answer is simple.

12
00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:51.786
How many days can we keep a riser connected
to this well, before that happens?

13
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.920
In this tutorial we will answer that
question exactly the way NORSOK U-001,

14
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.089
the 2026 edition, asks us to.

15
00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:05.549
We will derive every equation on the way,

16
00:01:05.589 --> 00:01:09.609
from the physics of a growing
crack to the design check,

17
00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:16.016
and at the end we will run the numbers
for a complete example, bin by bin.

18
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.316
Let's start with the hardware.

19
00:01:22.356 --> 00:01:25.886
At the top, the wellhead
connector of the blowout preventer

20
00:01:25.926 --> 00:01:28.998
latches onto the high
pressure wellhead housing.

21
00:01:29.038 --> 00:01:32.638
The top of that housing
is the wellhead datum,

22
00:01:32.798 --> 00:01:36.440
the reference point for
every load in this method.

23
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.690
The housing is welded
to a wellhead extension,

24
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.355
which continues down as
the 20 inch surface casing.

25
00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:46.945
Around it sits the low
pressure conductor housing,

26
00:01:47.025 --> 00:01:51.553
welded to a conductor extension
and the 36 inch conductor.

27
00:01:51.593 --> 00:01:55.983
The conductor housing lands in
a well slot of the template,

28
00:01:56.113 --> 00:01:58.695
a steel frame on the seabed.

29
00:01:58.975 --> 00:02:02.635
Below the mudline the conductor
is cemented into the soil,

30
00:02:02.765 --> 00:02:06.508
and the surface casing is
cemented inside the conductor.

31
00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:12.820
The standard defines the conductor as the
tubulars installed and cemented in a well,

32
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.692
providing support for, and load
sharing with, the surface casing.

33
00:02:17.732 --> 00:02:20.107
Notice the words load sharing.

34
00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:25.018
Most of this tutorial is
really about who carries what.

35
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:30.089
The rules are spread over
several parts of the standard.

36
00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:35.902
Clause 8.1 says that the marine drilling
riser loads in Annex A are the riser forces

37
00:02:35.942 --> 00:02:41.084
for the whole wellhead system:
wellhead housing, wellhead extension,

38
00:02:41.124 --> 00:02:44.098
conductor housing and conductor extension,

39
00:02:44.138 --> 00:02:49.739
and that the load share between wellhead
system and template shall be determined.

40
00:02:49.779 --> 00:02:55.289
Clause 13.3.6 covers the
structural design of the template,

41
00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:57.739
the conductor and the load share.

42
00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:01.144
Annex A gives us the fatigue loads.

43
00:03:01.184 --> 00:03:05.394
Annex B gives the design format
and the fatigue capacity.

44
00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:09.369
And Annex J describes the
load paths through the well.

45
00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:16.718
Two documents outside the standard do
the rest: DNV-RP-C203 for the S-N curves,

46
00:03:16.758 --> 00:03:22.924
and NORSOK N-001 for the
template's design fatigue factors.

47
00:03:24.183 --> 00:03:28.620
Here is the whole method
as a recipe of eight steps.

48
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.409
One, get the fatigue load
at the wellhead datum.

49
00:03:32.449 --> 00:03:36.362
Two, follow the load paths
and find the load share.

50
00:03:36.402 --> 00:03:40.269
Three, build a local
response model of the well.

51
00:03:40.309 --> 00:03:43.708
Four, convert moments
into hot spot stresses.

52
00:03:43.748 --> 00:03:47.616
Five, turn S-N curves into M-N curves.

53
00:03:47.656 --> 00:03:49.501
Six, sum the damage.

54
00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:52.010
Seven, apply the design factor.

55
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.707
And eight, screen, test the
sensitivities, and mitigate.

56
00:03:55.747 --> 00:04:00.206
Before we can do any of that,
we need one idea in our heads.

57
00:04:00.486 --> 00:04:02.508
What exactly is fatigue?

58
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.590
The standard defines fatigue
as a process of progressive,

59
00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.950
localized structural change,
under fluctuating stresses,

60
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:19.832
that can end in cracks or complete
fracture after enough load cycles.

61
00:04:19.872 --> 00:04:26.279
In a welded steel structure the story
almost always starts at a weld toe.

62
00:04:26.559 --> 00:04:30.995
The weld leaves a sharp notch,
tiny flaws and residual stress.

63
00:04:31.035 --> 00:04:35.241
Each cycle opens a microscopic
crack a tiny bit more.

64
00:04:35.281 --> 00:04:39.375
Millions of cycles later, the
crack is through the wall.

65
00:04:40.317 --> 00:04:43.352
Let's name the parts of a stress cycle.

66
00:04:43.392 --> 00:04:47.891
The stress range, Δσ, is the
maximum minus the minimum.

67
00:04:47.931 --> 00:04:52.503
The amplitude is half of that,
and the mean sits in the middle.

68
00:04:52.543 --> 00:04:56.719
The ratio of minimum
to maximum is called R.

69
00:04:56.759 --> 00:05:01.038
NORSOK asks for fully reversed
loading, R equal to minus one,

70
00:05:01.078 --> 00:05:04.757
so the cycle swings
symmetrically about zero.

71
00:05:06.017 --> 00:05:09.872
Why do we only care about
the range, and not the mean?

72
00:05:09.912 --> 00:05:14.398
Because a fresh weld carries residual
tensile stress close to yield.

73
00:05:14.438 --> 00:05:16.302
Whatever the nominal mean is,

74
00:05:16.342 --> 00:05:22.540
the material right at the toe is cycling
down from that high tensile level.

75
00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.980
Two cycles with the same
range do the same damage.

76
00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.747
That is why every S-N
curve for welded joints

77
00:05:30.787 --> 00:05:35.138
is written in terms of
the stress range alone.

78
00:05:36.083 --> 00:05:39.294
Now, where does the S-N curve come from?

79
00:05:39.334 --> 00:05:40.871
Let's derive it.

80
00:05:40.911 --> 00:05:44.575
A fatigue crack grows a
small amount every cycle.

81
00:05:44.615 --> 00:05:48.618
Experiments show that the
growth per cycle, d a by d N,

82
00:05:48.658 --> 00:05:54.922
is proportional to the stress intensity
range, delta K, raised to a power m.

83
00:05:54.962 --> 00:05:57.047
This is the Paris law.

84
00:05:57.087 --> 00:06:00.327
The stress intensity
range is the stress range,

85
00:06:00.437 --> 00:06:06.157
times the square root of pi times the
crack depth, times a geometry factor Y.

86
00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:14.198
Substitute delta K into the Paris law and
separate the variables: d N equals d a,

87
00:06:14.238 --> 00:06:19.672
divided by C, times Y to
the m, times Δσ to the m,

88
00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:22.347
times pi a to the power m over two.

89
00:06:22.387 --> 00:06:27.341
Now integrate from the initial
flaw size to the final crack size.

90
00:06:27.721 --> 00:06:34.310
The integral depends only on the geometry
and the flaw sizes, not on the load.

91
00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.673
So it is just a constant.

92
00:06:36.713 --> 00:06:39.862
And what is left is beautifully simple.

93
00:06:40.142 --> 00:06:46.319
The number of cycles, times the stress
range to the power m, is a constant.

94
00:06:46.359 --> 00:06:49.068
We call that constant a bar.

95
00:06:50.017 --> 00:06:55.377
Take the logarithm of both sides
and you get log N equals log a bar,

96
00:06:55.517 --> 00:06:58.517
minus m times log Δσ.

97
00:06:58.557 --> 00:07:02.140
On log log paper that is a straight line.

98
00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.049
The slope m is the inverse
slope of the S-N curve,

99
00:07:06.089 --> 00:07:09.480
and for welded steel it is about three,

100
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.229
the same exponent the
Paris law gives for steel.

101
00:07:14.269 --> 00:07:20.455
A doubling of the stress range divides
the life by two to the power three.

102
00:07:20.495 --> 00:07:22.047
Eight times shorter.

103
00:07:22.983 --> 00:07:24.883
Real test data scatter.

104
00:07:24.923 --> 00:07:29.983
At a given stress range, log N
follows roughly a normal distribution.

105
00:07:30.023 --> 00:07:35.979
The design S-N curve is placed two
standard deviations below the mean.

106
00:07:36.019 --> 00:07:43.760
As NORSOK puts it, 2.3 % of the test values
are expected to fall below the curve.

107
00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.238
A 97.7 % probability of survival.

108
00:07:49.183 --> 00:07:55.885
NORSOK sends us to DNV-RP-C203
for the curves themselves.

109
00:07:55.925 --> 00:07:57.925
They come in families.

110
00:07:57.965 --> 00:08:01.955
In air, the curve bends
at 10 million cycles,

111
00:08:02.025 --> 00:08:05.317
from slope three to a flatter slope five.

112
00:08:05.357 --> 00:08:11.456
In seawater with cathodic protection, the
bend comes earlier, at 1 million cycles.

113
00:08:11.496 --> 00:08:15.258
And under free corrosion
there is no bend at all.

114
00:08:15.298 --> 00:08:20.576
We will use the C1 curve in
seawater with cathodic protection.

115
00:08:20.856 --> 00:08:29.250
For fewer than a million cycles, m
one is three and log a bar is 12.049.

116
00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:36.488
Above a million cycles, m two
is five and log a bar is 16.081.

117
00:08:36.528 --> 00:08:41.560
These are the values from the
2016 edition, for teaching.

118
00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.917
The 2024 edition that NORSOK refers
to revised the seawater curves,

119
00:08:46.957 --> 00:08:52.409
so always take design values from
the edition in your design basis.

120
00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.913
One more correction.

121
00:08:54.953 --> 00:08:59.133
Thick sections are weaker
in fatigue than thin ones,

122
00:08:59.283 --> 00:09:04.919
because the stress at the weld toe decays
more slowly through the thickness and

123
00:09:04.959 --> 00:09:07.468
there is more volume for flaws.

124
00:09:07.508 --> 00:09:10.928
The S-N curve handles this
with a thickness factor:

125
00:09:11.198 --> 00:09:16.148
the stress range is multiplied
by t over t ref, to the power k,

126
00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:19.511
with t ref equal to 25 millimetres.

127
00:09:19.551 --> 00:09:25.061
For our 2 inch conductor
wall and k equal to 0.15,

128
00:09:25.101 --> 00:09:29.741
the stress is scaled up by about 11 %.

129
00:09:30.983 --> 00:09:35.092
And here is a direct
requirement from the standard.

130
00:09:35.132 --> 00:09:36.765
Girth welds on the conductor,

131
00:09:36.805 --> 00:09:42.938
for example the weld between the conductor
housing and the conductor extension,

132
00:09:42.978 --> 00:09:44.691
shall meet S-N class C1.

133
00:09:44.731 --> 00:09:49.124
C1 sits above the classes you would
give an ordinary as-welded joint,

134
00:09:49.164 --> 00:09:53.151
so the weld profile, the
fit-up tolerances and the

135
00:09:53.191 --> 00:09:56.783
inspection have to be
good enough to justify it.

136
00:10:00.917 --> 00:10:04.377
Step one of the method
is the fatigue load.

137
00:10:04.417 --> 00:10:05.907
Follow the chain.

138
00:10:05.947 --> 00:10:07.913
Waves move the rig.

139
00:10:07.953 --> 00:10:11.573
The rig drags the top of the riser around.

140
00:10:11.613 --> 00:10:17.073
The riser rotates in the lower flex
joint just above the stack, and the tall,

141
00:10:17.113 --> 00:10:22.361
heavy BOP stack sways on the
wellhead like an inverted pendulum.

142
00:10:22.401 --> 00:10:28.772
All of that arrives at the wellhead datum
as a bending moment and a shear force.

143
00:10:29.717 --> 00:10:34.239
Let's derive the relation
between the moment and the shear.

144
00:10:34.279 --> 00:10:37.727
Treat the lower flex joint
as a hinge that passes

145
00:10:37.767 --> 00:10:41.433
a horizontal force Q but almost no moment.

146
00:10:41.473 --> 00:10:44.943
It sits a height H above
the wellhead datum.

147
00:10:44.983 --> 00:10:50.934
Take moments about the datum, and
the moment there is simply Q times H.

148
00:10:51.214 --> 00:10:55.915
So the shear at the datum
is the moment divided by H.

149
00:10:55.955 --> 00:11:02.090
That is exactly the formula in the
footnotes to tables A four and A five.

150
00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:07.853
For the standard design
case, H is up to 13.7 metres.

151
00:11:08.133 --> 00:11:14.496
With the BOP on top of a christmas tree,
the lever grows by the height of the tree,

152
00:11:14.536 --> 00:11:17.858
up to another 2.5 metres.

153
00:11:18.817 --> 00:11:22.648
Now cut the well at a
depth z below the datum.

154
00:11:22.688 --> 00:11:27.418
The same force Q now has
a longer lever, H plus z.

155
00:11:27.458 --> 00:11:32.648
So the moment is Mw
times one plus z over H.

156
00:11:32.688 --> 00:11:36.694
This is why the standard
says that the moment below

157
00:11:36.734 --> 00:11:40.434
the datum shall be increased
due to the shear force.

158
00:11:40.474 --> 00:11:44.964
In our example the mudline
is 4 metres below the datum,

159
00:11:45.124 --> 00:11:50.584
so the moment there is already
29 % bigger than at the datum.

160
00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:55.207
Annex A gives two kinds of riser load.

161
00:11:55.247 --> 00:11:58.517
The extreme and accidental
moments in tables

162
00:11:58.557 --> 00:12:01.867
A four and A five are for static design.

163
00:12:01.907 --> 00:12:06.366
For fatigue, the standard specifies
a single load bending moment range,

164
00:12:06.406 --> 00:12:09.721
and a general bending moment histogram.

165
00:12:09.761 --> 00:12:11.643
Let's look at both.

166
00:12:12.583 --> 00:12:18.603
Table A.7 gives the annual single
load bending moment range, ΔMo,

167
00:12:18.643 --> 00:12:20.146
at the wellhead datum.

168
00:12:20.186 --> 00:12:22.376
It depends on the water depth,

169
00:12:22.496 --> 00:12:27.196
and on whether the BOP sits
on the wellhead or on a tree.

170
00:12:27.236 --> 00:12:31.639
Notice that shallow water
is the worst case: a short,

171
00:12:31.679 --> 00:12:36.936
stiff riser passes more of the
vessel motion down to the well.

172
00:12:36.976 --> 00:12:40.816
And the tree mode is always
worse than the wellhead mode,

173
00:12:40.946 --> 00:12:44.099
because the stack is taller and heavier.

174
00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.404
Table A.8 is the shape of the year.

175
00:12:48.444 --> 00:12:54.011
It lists normalised moment
ranges from 0.1 to 2.9,

176
00:12:54.051 --> 00:12:57.947
and how many cycles of
each happen in one year.

177
00:12:57.987 --> 00:13:04.007
Small cycles are extremely common:
almost 4 million cycles at 0.1.

178
00:13:04.047 --> 00:13:07.753
The biggest cycles happen
once or twice a year.

179
00:13:07.793 --> 00:13:14.607
In total that is 7.47 million cycles per
year, roughly one every four seconds.

180
00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:18.830
To get the histogram for a real project,

181
00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:25.698
you multiply every range in table A.8
by the single load range from table A.7.

182
00:13:25.738 --> 00:13:30.858
The standard's own example:
for 80 to 250 metres of water,

183
00:13:30.898 --> 00:13:39.777
0.1 times 2124 gives 212 kilonewton
metres, 3.9 million times a year.

184
00:13:39.817 --> 00:13:45.897
Our example well is in 350 metres of
water with the BOP on the wellhead,

185
00:13:46.087 --> 00:13:54.337
so ΔMo is 1436 kilonewton metres,
and the bins run from about 140,

186
00:13:54.427 --> 00:13:57.516
up to over 4000 kilonewton metres.

187
00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.132
Two more rules from Annex A.

188
00:14:01.172 --> 00:14:06.122
The fatigue service life is the fatigue
life divided by the design factor,

189
00:14:06.342 --> 00:14:09.255
and it is equivalent to
the permissible number of

190
00:14:09.295 --> 00:14:13.601
operational days with a
BOP and riser connected.

191
00:14:13.641 --> 00:14:16.741
For both the template
and the wellhead system,

192
00:14:16.791 --> 00:14:20.572
the minimum calculated fatigue
service life is one year.

193
00:14:20.612 --> 00:14:25.772
And the histogram does not include
vortex induced vibration of the riser.

194
00:14:26.052 --> 00:14:30.095
If that is relevant, its
damage must be added.

195
00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:35.449
Step two.

196
00:14:35.489 --> 00:14:38.850
Where does the moment go
once it enters the well?

197
00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:42.065
Annex J answers with eight contact areas.

198
00:14:42.105 --> 00:14:45.055
F, where the connector meets the wellhead.

199
00:14:45.095 --> 00:14:50.505
A and B, where the wellhead housing
bears against the conductor housing,

200
00:14:50.545 --> 00:14:52.753
at the top and at the bottom.

201
00:14:52.793 --> 00:14:57.669
C and D, where the conductor housing
bears against the template slot.

202
00:14:57.709 --> 00:15:00.939
G, where the template sits on the seabed.

203
00:15:00.979 --> 00:15:05.500
E, the top of the cement between
conductor and surface casing.

204
00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:09.563
And H, the conductor
against the soil and cement.

205
00:15:10.517 --> 00:15:14.663
Through those contacts the load
can travel along three paths.

206
00:15:14.703 --> 00:15:20.043
Path one goes down the wellhead
extension and the surface casing,

207
00:15:20.243 --> 00:15:23.670
and into the soil below
the top of the cement.

208
00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:28.052
Path two jumps across to the
conductor housing at A and B,

209
00:15:28.092 --> 00:15:31.828
and goes down the conductor into the soil.

210
00:15:31.868 --> 00:15:37.278
Path three goes from the conductor
housing into the template at C and D,

211
00:15:37.428 --> 00:15:39.986
and through the template into the seabed.

212
00:15:40.026 --> 00:15:44.873
The standard says the load sharing
depends on tolerances and on the relative

213
00:15:44.913 --> 00:15:48.560
stiffness of all the
elements, including the soil.

214
00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.915
So let's understand stiffness.

215
00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.947
Picture two springs side
by side under a rigid bar.

216
00:15:55.987 --> 00:15:59.142
Push the bar down with a force F.

217
00:15:59.182 --> 00:16:03.188
Both springs must move by
the same amount, delta.

218
00:16:03.228 --> 00:16:05.022
That is compatibility.

219
00:16:05.062 --> 00:16:09.211
Each spring pushes back with
its stiffness times delta.

220
00:16:09.251 --> 00:16:11.479
That is the material law.

221
00:16:11.519 --> 00:16:15.089
And the two forces must add up to F.

222
00:16:15.129 --> 00:16:17.020
That is equilibrium.

223
00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.332
Solve for delta, put it back,

224
00:16:19.372 --> 00:16:25.417
and the share of each spring is its
stiffness divided by the total stiffness.

225
00:16:25.697 --> 00:16:27.492
Stiffness attracts load.

226
00:16:27.532 --> 00:16:32.303
That one line is the core of
every load share analysis.

227
00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:38.211
Vertically, the well and the template's
foundation are two springs in parallel.

228
00:16:38.251 --> 00:16:42.271
Table 4 gives indicative shares
carried by the structure:

229
00:16:42.501 --> 00:16:46.461
from 50 % for a single
caisson down to 20 %

230
00:16:46.501 --> 00:16:49.509
for the centre slot of
a six slot template.

231
00:16:49.549 --> 00:16:54.619
Under the fully supporting conductor
methodology, which is the main principle,

232
00:16:54.779 --> 00:16:58.183
the conductor is designed to
carry all vertical hang-off loads,

233
00:16:58.223 --> 00:17:02.743
while the template is still designed
for its stiffness based share.

234
00:17:03.023 --> 00:17:07.811
Each element is designed for
the share that is worst for it.

235
00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.522
The same idea works for bending.

236
00:17:11.562 --> 00:17:13.742
Below the top of the cement,

237
00:17:13.782 --> 00:17:17.842
the conductor and the surface
casing are glued together,

238
00:17:18.012 --> 00:17:21.849
so they must bend to the
same curvature, kappa.

239
00:17:21.889 --> 00:17:26.605
Each string resists with its own
bending stiffness, EI, times kappa,

240
00:17:26.645 --> 00:17:28.656
and the moments add up.

241
00:17:28.696 --> 00:17:33.586
So each string takes its
EI divided by the total EI.

242
00:17:33.626 --> 00:17:38.446
For a 36 by 2 inch conductor
around a 20 by 1 inch casing,

243
00:17:38.596 --> 00:17:41.849
the conductor takes about 92 %.

244
00:17:42.129 --> 00:17:46.309
But above the top of the
cement, in the cement shortfall,

245
00:17:46.349 --> 00:17:48.537
the strings are not bonded.

246
00:17:48.817 --> 00:17:52.417
The surface casing must
carry its share on its own,

247
00:17:52.587 --> 00:17:55.907
which is why the standard
calls cement shortfall

248
00:17:55.947 --> 00:17:59.017
one of the main factors
in wellhead fatigue.

249
00:17:59.983 --> 00:18:02.539
Now the conductor template interface.

250
00:18:02.579 --> 00:18:09.081
The slot holds the conductor housing at
two rings, C and D, a distance h apart.

251
00:18:09.121 --> 00:18:13.061
If the housing tilts
by a small angle theta,

252
00:18:13.131 --> 00:18:19.714
one ring is pushed by theta times h
over two, the other the opposite way.

253
00:18:19.754 --> 00:18:22.084
The two ring forces form a couple,

254
00:18:22.124 --> 00:18:26.134
and the couple divided by theta
is a rotational stiffness:

255
00:18:26.304 --> 00:18:28.622
k times h squared over two.

256
00:18:28.662 --> 00:18:32.632
With different ring stiffnesses, it
becomes the product over the sum,

257
00:18:32.672 --> 00:18:34.326
times h squared.

258
00:18:34.366 --> 00:18:39.786
For rings of 200 and 400 meganewtons
per metre, 2 metres apart,

259
00:18:39.856 --> 00:18:44.156
that is about 530 meganewton
metres per radian.

260
00:18:44.436 --> 00:18:49.667
The template and the well below it are
two rotational springs in parallel,

261
00:18:49.707 --> 00:18:54.055
and the moment splits by
the same stiffness rule.

262
00:18:54.095 --> 00:18:57.325
But real slots have a radial clearance.

263
00:18:57.605 --> 00:19:02.116
Until the gap closes, the
template carries nothing at all.

264
00:19:02.156 --> 00:19:08.435
So the share depends on the size of the
cycle, and that makes the problem nonlinear.

265
00:19:09.383 --> 00:19:12.386
Vertically, the interface
has a different job.

266
00:19:12.426 --> 00:19:15.647
A producing well heats up and grows.

267
00:19:15.687 --> 00:19:19.687
The free growth of a steel string
is the expansion coefficient,

268
00:19:19.767 --> 00:19:23.055
times the temperature rise,
times the free length.

269
00:19:23.095 --> 00:19:28.715
12 millionths per degree, times
60 degrees, times 120 metres,

270
00:19:28.755 --> 00:19:31.017
is about 86 millimetres.

271
00:19:31.057 --> 00:19:36.497
If you stopped that growth completely,
the force would be E A alpha delta T,

272
00:19:36.647 --> 00:19:40.121
almost 6 meganewtons in
the surface casing alone.

273
00:19:40.161 --> 00:19:44.965
So the standard requires the template
design to accommodate a relative vertical

274
00:19:45.005 --> 00:19:49.871
displacement of 100 millimetres
between template and conductor,

275
00:19:50.031 --> 00:19:52.784
including thermal growth and settlement.

276
00:19:53.064 --> 00:19:58.619
The slot must let the well slide
vertically, and still hold it laterally.

277
00:20:02.783 --> 00:20:07.958
Below the mudline, the conductor
is a beam pushed against the soil.

278
00:20:07.998 --> 00:20:12.548
The simplest model treats the soil
as a bed of independent springs:

279
00:20:12.828 --> 00:20:15.958
the soil pushes back with
a force per metre equal

280
00:20:15.998 --> 00:20:20.100
to a modulus k times the
lateral deflection y.

281
00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:23.071
Take a short slice of the conductor.

282
00:20:23.351 --> 00:20:28.274
The change in shear force along
the slice equals the soil reaction.

283
00:20:28.314 --> 00:20:31.460
The change in moment equals the shear.

284
00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:35.031
And the moment is EI times the curvature.

285
00:20:35.071 --> 00:20:40.411
Put the three together and you get
EI times the fourth derivative of y,

286
00:20:40.531 --> 00:20:44.220
plus k times y, equals zero.

287
00:20:45.183 --> 00:20:48.998
Try a solution of the
form e to the lambda z.

288
00:20:49.038 --> 00:20:54.706
The equation becomes EI lambda
to the fourth plus k equals zero.

289
00:20:54.746 --> 00:20:59.766
If we define beta as the
fourth root of k over four EI,

290
00:20:59.906 --> 00:21:06.552
the four roots are beta times plus
or minus one, plus or minus i.

291
00:21:06.592 --> 00:21:10.495
Far down the conductor the
deflection has to die out,

292
00:21:10.535 --> 00:21:12.842
so we keep only the two decaying roots:

293
00:21:13.022 --> 00:21:17.195
an exponential decay
times a cosine and a sine.

294
00:21:17.475 --> 00:21:23.512
One over beta is a length, the distance
over which the conductor feels the soil.

295
00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:30.332
At the mudline, the stick-up hands
over a moment M zero and a shear P.

296
00:21:30.372 --> 00:21:34.062
Differentiate the solution
twice and three times,

297
00:21:34.132 --> 00:21:39.221
set the moment and the shear at the
mudline, and the two constants fall out.

298
00:21:39.261 --> 00:21:44.321
The moment down the conductor is M
zero times e to the minus beta x,

299
00:21:44.411 --> 00:21:51.775
times cosine plus sine, plus P over beta,
times e to the minus beta x, times sine.

300
00:21:52.055 --> 00:21:55.646
A decaying, slightly
oscillating wave of bending.

301
00:21:56.583 --> 00:21:58.809
Where is the moment largest?

302
00:21:58.849 --> 00:22:03.909
Set the derivative to zero, and
the tangent of beta x equals P,

303
00:22:03.969 --> 00:22:07.247
divided by two beta M zero plus P.

304
00:22:07.287 --> 00:22:11.647
For our conductor in soil
with k equal to 5 megapascals,

305
00:22:11.737 --> 00:22:19.157
one over beta is 6.8 metres, and the
peak is 1.33 times the wellhead moment,

306
00:22:19.227 --> 00:22:21.703
about 1 metre below the mudline.

307
00:22:21.983 --> 00:22:27.934
In softer soil the peak moves deeper,
and the bending reaches further down.

308
00:22:27.974 --> 00:22:33.414
The same solution also tells us how stiff
the well is in rotation at the mudline:

309
00:22:33.714 --> 00:22:39.276
EI times beta, about 390
meganewton metres per radian.

310
00:22:39.556 --> 00:22:43.406
Compare that with the template's 530,

311
00:22:43.526 --> 00:22:48.876
and in this simple picture the template
would take more than half of the moment,

312
00:22:48.916 --> 00:22:50.679
if there were no gap.

313
00:22:51.617 --> 00:22:54.131
Real soil is less tidy.

314
00:22:54.171 --> 00:22:59.305
Its resistance is nonlinear, grows
with depth and softens under cycling.

315
00:22:59.345 --> 00:23:03.602
And the interface depends on
how the conductor went in.

316
00:23:03.642 --> 00:23:05.952
Driven gives the best contact,

317
00:23:06.012 --> 00:23:09.932
while a drilled and cemented
conductor is the least predictable,

318
00:23:09.992 --> 00:23:12.479
with the poorest horizontal support.

319
00:23:12.519 --> 00:23:17.160
That is why NORSOK does not
rely on one hand calculation.

320
00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:20.180
It asks for a local response model,

321
00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:25.274
and for the fatigue capacity to be
determined over a range of parameters.

322
00:23:29.417 --> 00:23:32.091
Step four turns moments into stresses.

323
00:23:32.131 --> 00:23:34.004
Start with beam theory.

324
00:23:34.044 --> 00:23:39.124
Plane sections stay plane, so the strain
grows linearly with the distance y from the

325
00:23:39.164 --> 00:23:43.408
neutral axis: strain
equals curvature times y.

326
00:23:43.448 --> 00:23:47.213
Steel is elastic, so
stress is E times strain.

327
00:23:47.253 --> 00:23:53.448
Add up the moment of all those stresses
over the cross section and you get E times

328
00:23:53.488 --> 00:23:58.676
kappa times the integral of
y squared, which is EI kappa.

329
00:23:58.716 --> 00:24:03.087
Eliminate kappa, and the
stress is M y over I.

330
00:24:03.367 --> 00:24:09.159
At the outer surface, it is M
divided by the section modulus W.

331
00:24:10.117 --> 00:24:13.841
For a tube, the trick is
to integrate in rings.

332
00:24:13.881 --> 00:24:18.921
The polar moment, the integral
of r squared over the area,

333
00:24:19.091 --> 00:24:24.934
is pi over two times r outer to the
fourth minus r inner to the fourth.

334
00:24:24.974 --> 00:24:29.750
By symmetry, the bending moment
of inertia is half of that.

335
00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:35.665
In diameters: pi over 64, times D
to the fourth minus d to the fourth.

336
00:24:35.705 --> 00:24:43.418
For our conductor, 914 millimetres
outside and 813 inside,

337
00:24:43.458 --> 00:24:48.122
I is 1.29 times 10¹⁰
millimetres to the fourth,

338
00:24:48.162 --> 00:24:53.373
and W is 2.82 times 10⁷ cubic millimetres.

339
00:24:54.617 --> 00:24:57.632
Here is our first hot spot, GW1,

340
00:24:57.672 --> 00:25:02.567
the girth weld between the conductor
housing and the conductor extension,

341
00:25:02.607 --> 00:25:04.658
3 metres below the datum.

342
00:25:04.698 --> 00:25:10.408
When the wellhead sees a moment
range of 3000 kilonewton metres,

343
00:25:10.588 --> 00:25:14.628
the shear lever pushes the
moment up on the way down,

344
00:25:14.748 --> 00:25:19.201
and the load share to the template
and the casing pulls it back.

345
00:25:19.241 --> 00:25:26.658
The local model says the conductor carries
2583 kilonewton metres at this weld.

346
00:25:26.698 --> 00:25:32.925
Divide by W, and the nominal
stress range is 91.6 megapascals.

347
00:25:33.883 --> 00:25:36.789
The weld itself raises the stress.

348
00:25:36.829 --> 00:25:40.930
The most important reason in
a girth weld is misalignment.

349
00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:44.694
Offset two plates by e and
pull them with a force N:

350
00:25:44.734 --> 00:25:52.703
the line of force has to jog sideways, which
creates a moment N times e at the weld.

351
00:25:52.743 --> 00:25:56.663
If both sides restrain it
equally, each takes half,

352
00:25:56.763 --> 00:26:00.968
and the extra bending stress
is three N e over t squared.

353
00:26:01.008 --> 00:26:05.903
So the stress concentration
factor is one plus three e over t.

354
00:26:05.943 --> 00:26:10.023
A tube restrains the offset
better than a flat plate,

355
00:26:10.143 --> 00:26:13.630
and DNV gives the tubular version.

356
00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:18.700
In our example we use an SCF of 1.10,

357
00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:23.997
so the hot spot stress
range is 100.7 megapascals.

358
00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:29.630
Our second hot spot,
TS1, is in the template:

359
00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:34.021
the weld where the upper slot
ring meets its support beams.

360
00:26:34.061 --> 00:26:37.271
The ring force is shared by two beams,

361
00:26:37.411 --> 00:26:41.491
each fixed at both ends
and loaded at mid span.

362
00:26:41.531 --> 00:26:48.659
A fixed-fixed beam with a central load P
has a mid span moment of P L over eight,

363
00:26:48.699 --> 00:26:51.884
so each beam sees F L over 16.

364
00:26:51.924 --> 00:26:59.751
With an SCF of two, that gives 0.156
megapascals per kilonewton of ring force.

365
00:26:59.791 --> 00:27:07.161
At a wellhead moment range of 3000, the
ring force range is 598 kilonewtons,

366
00:27:07.291 --> 00:27:11.491
and the hot spot stress
range is 93 megapascals.

367
00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:18.390
In a real assessment, the critical regions
are found by finite element analysis,

368
00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:21.358
and the standard notes
that four to six hot spots

369
00:27:21.398 --> 00:27:24.796
with the lowest capacity
are usually enough.

370
00:27:24.836 --> 00:27:29.026
Typical candidates are the
wellhead housing to extension weld,

371
00:27:29.146 --> 00:27:33.776
the surface casing near the top of
the cement, the conductor girth welds,

372
00:27:33.816 --> 00:27:35.812
and the template slot welds.

373
00:27:35.852 --> 00:27:41.434
We follow two of them
all the way: GW1 and TS1.

374
00:27:45.583 --> 00:27:46.870
Step five.

375
00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:50.970
The load histogram is
written in wellhead moments,

376
00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:54.256
but the S-N curve is written in stress.

377
00:27:54.296 --> 00:27:59.847
So NORSOK asks us to express the capacity
directly in moments: an M-N curve,

378
00:27:59.887 --> 00:28:05.653
the number of cycles to failure against
the moment range at the wellhead datum.

379
00:28:05.693 --> 00:28:11.296
Load and capacity then live at the same
point, and comparing them becomes trivial.

380
00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:19.050
If the stress were simply proportional to
the moment, Δσ equal to kappa times ΔM,

381
00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:21.375
the conversion would be one line.

382
00:28:21.415 --> 00:28:29.983
Put it into the S-N curve: N equals a bar
sigma, times kappa ΔM, to the minus m.

383
00:28:30.023 --> 00:28:34.913
Group the constants, and you get
the same power law in the moment,

384
00:28:35.063 --> 00:28:40.994
with a new intercept, a bar
sigma times kappa to the minus m.

385
00:28:41.034 --> 00:28:45.373
That is formula B.8,
and in logarithms, B.9.

386
00:28:45.413 --> 00:28:47.597
The slope does not change.

387
00:28:47.637 --> 00:28:49.636
Only the intercept moves.

388
00:28:50.583 --> 00:28:55.391
But in a wellhead, the stress is
not proportional to the moment.

389
00:28:55.431 --> 00:29:00.023
Contacts open and close, gaps
take up, and preload is overcome.

390
00:29:00.063 --> 00:29:06.470
Look at our girth weld: the curve bends
over as the template slot starts to help.

391
00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:12.554
And the template weld sees nothing at
all until the 2 millimetre gap closes.

392
00:29:12.594 --> 00:29:18.638
That is why the standard requires finite
element analysis to find the stress ranges.

393
00:29:19.583 --> 00:29:25.246
The procedure in clause
B.4.2.4 goes like this.

394
00:29:25.286 --> 00:29:30.196
A, build a three dimensional elastic
model with all the contacts, preloads,

395
00:29:30.236 --> 00:29:34.441
cement shortfall, friction,
tolerances and clearances.

396
00:29:34.481 --> 00:29:40.924
B, apply a moment at the datum, together
with its shear, the moment over H.

397
00:29:40.964 --> 00:29:44.499
C, apply it plus, then
minus, fully reversed,

398
00:29:44.539 --> 00:29:48.014
and compute the stress
range at every hot spot,

399
00:29:48.114 --> 00:29:50.851
iterating until the contacts converge.

400
00:29:50.891 --> 00:29:55.323
D, read the cycles to
failure from the S-N curve.

401
00:29:55.363 --> 00:29:58.703
E, report those cycles
against the moment range.

402
00:29:58.743 --> 00:30:07.342
And f, repeat with different moment
levels until you cover 10⁴ to 10⁸ cycles.

403
00:30:07.382 --> 00:30:11.112
The standard suggests six
levels, halving each time,

404
00:30:11.152 --> 00:30:16.310
from 6000 down to 187.5 kilonewton metres.

405
00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:22.550
For this tutorial we built a simplified
version of that model, two beam strings,

406
00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:28.040
the wellhead and casing inside, the
conductor housing and conductor outside,

407
00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:33.340
joined at the contacts, supported by the
template rings with a 2 millimetre gap,

408
00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:37.996
glued together below the top of the
cement, and resting on soil springs.

409
00:30:38.036 --> 00:30:42.256
It is a teaching stand-in for
the three dimensional model,

410
00:30:42.376 --> 00:30:46.325
but it follows the same
steps, so let's run them.

411
00:30:47.283 --> 00:30:50.552
Six moment ranges, plus and minus each.

412
00:30:50.592 --> 00:30:54.644
At 6000 kilonewton metres
the girth weld sees 200

413
00:30:54.684 --> 00:30:59.673
megapascals and fails
after about 100 000 cycles.

414
00:30:59.713 --> 00:31:05.402
At 3000, a hundred megapascals,
and about 800 000 cycles.

415
00:31:05.442 --> 00:31:11.331
At the low end, the stresses are a few
megapascals and the lives run into the

416
00:31:11.371 --> 00:31:14.220
hundreds of billions of cycles.

417
00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:17.370
The template weld
follows the same pattern,

418
00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:22.851
but it falls away much faster at small
moments, because there the gap is open.

419
00:31:24.117 --> 00:31:26.687
Because the S-N curve has two slopes,

420
00:31:26.727 --> 00:31:32.077
the natural capacity format is a
bilinear M-N curve, formula B.10:

421
00:31:32.357 --> 00:31:37.075
slope m one for large moments,
slope m two for small ones.

422
00:31:37.115 --> 00:31:39.117
Where do the branches meet?

423
00:31:39.157 --> 00:31:42.408
Set the two expressions for log N equal.

424
00:31:42.448 --> 00:31:47.658
The log of the moment at the knee
is the difference of the intercepts,

425
00:31:47.728 --> 00:31:50.814
divided by the difference of the slopes.

426
00:31:50.854 --> 00:31:53.250
That is formula B.11.

427
00:31:54.183 --> 00:32:00.878
The standard asks for a lower bound, a
capacity curve at or below every point.

428
00:32:00.918 --> 00:32:05.240
A simple way is to keep the
S-N slopes, three and five,

429
00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:10.676
and slide each branch down
until no point lies below it.

430
00:32:10.716 --> 00:32:14.784
In logs, each intercept is
the smallest value of log

431
00:32:14.824 --> 00:32:19.340
N plus m log ΔM over the
points of that branch.

432
00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:27.920
For GW1 that gives 16.33 for the steep
branch, 22.75 for the flat branch,

433
00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:31.736
and a knee at about
1600 kilonewton metres.

434
00:32:32.016 --> 00:32:36.626
Notice that the flat branch
is set by the smallest moment,

435
00:32:36.746 --> 00:32:40.809
where the gap is open and
the conductor works alone.

436
00:32:40.849 --> 00:32:45.307
The lower bound protects you from
exactly that kind of nonlinearity.

437
00:32:46.250 --> 00:32:48.812
Two more items complete the capacity.

438
00:32:48.852 --> 00:32:54.332
The M-N curve shall include a
fatigue life reduction factor, RF,

439
00:32:54.372 --> 00:32:59.153
from DNV-RP-C203 section 5.7.

440
00:32:59.193 --> 00:33:05.994
It divides the cycles, so it
simply lowers log a bar by log RF.

441
00:33:06.034 --> 00:33:11.445
We keep it at one in this example
so the arithmetic stays visible.

442
00:33:11.485 --> 00:33:16.012
Just remember that the life
scales with one over RF.

443
00:33:16.052 --> 00:33:19.532
And the standard asks you to tabulate ΔMc,

444
00:33:19.662 --> 00:33:23.940
the single load fatigue
capacity at 1 million cycles.

445
00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:30.125
For our girth weld it is
about 2780 kilonewton metres,

446
00:33:30.165 --> 00:33:35.558
and for the template weld about 2840.

447
00:33:39.717 --> 00:33:41.982
Step six is the damage.

448
00:33:42.022 --> 00:33:49.886
If a moment range gives failure after N i
cycles, then one cycle uses up a fraction,

449
00:33:49.926 --> 00:33:52.401
one over N i, of the life.

450
00:33:52.441 --> 00:33:57.733
N i cycles use up n i over N i.

451
00:33:57.773 --> 00:34:02.853
The Palmgren Miner rule simply adds
these fractions over all the bins,

452
00:34:02.963 --> 00:34:05.750
ignoring the order in
which the cycles come.

453
00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:09.237
Failure is predicted
when the sum reaches one.

454
00:34:10.183 --> 00:34:15.983
Our histogram covers one year, so
the damage is a damage per year,

455
00:34:16.173 --> 00:34:20.915
and the fatigue life in years
is simply one over that damage.

456
00:34:20.955 --> 00:34:23.825
The design format, formula B.3,

457
00:34:23.955 --> 00:34:26.902
then says that the service
life must not exceed the

458
00:34:26.942 --> 00:34:30.611
fatigue life divided by
the design fatigue factor.

459
00:34:30.891 --> 00:34:33.819
The same format sets inspection intervals.

460
00:34:33.859 --> 00:34:38.599
And table B.4 gives the factor:
10 for permanent equipment,

461
00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:40.635
which is assumed never to be inspected,

462
00:34:40.675 --> 00:34:46.826
three for the service life of temporary
equipment, and 10 for inspection intervals.

463
00:34:47.783 --> 00:34:52.303
For the template, Annex A
sends us to NORSOK N-001,

464
00:34:52.343 --> 00:34:56.618
where the design fatigue factor
depends on two questions.

465
00:34:56.658 --> 00:34:59.888
Can the detail be inspected and repaired?

466
00:34:59.928 --> 00:35:03.295
And would its failure have
substantial consequences?

467
00:35:03.335 --> 00:35:08.045
A slot weld hidden behind the
conductor housing cannot be inspected,

468
00:35:08.085 --> 00:35:11.823
and losing it would take
away support from the well.

469
00:35:11.863 --> 00:35:13.508
So we use 10.

470
00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:19.399
Now let's derive the remaining
formulas of clause B.4.4.

471
00:35:19.439 --> 00:35:27.859
With a linear M-N curve, the damage
is the sum of n i times ΔM i to the m,

472
00:35:27.949 --> 00:35:30.058
divided by a bar.

473
00:35:30.098 --> 00:35:36.035
Ask: what single moment range, applied for
all n cycles, would do the same damage?

474
00:35:36.075 --> 00:35:38.524
Set the two damages equal.

475
00:35:38.564 --> 00:35:45.594
The a bar cancels, and the equivalent
range is the average of ΔM to the m,

476
00:35:45.754 --> 00:35:48.535
raised to the power one over m.

477
00:35:48.575 --> 00:35:50.828
That is formula B.14.

478
00:35:50.868 --> 00:35:54.088
For our histogram and m equal to three,

479
00:35:54.248 --> 00:36:02.936
7.47 million cycles at 683 kilonewton
metres do the same damage as the whole year.

480
00:36:04.183 --> 00:36:07.036
The standard goes one step further.

481
00:36:07.076 --> 00:36:11.795
Draw a demand curve with slope
m through that equivalent point.

482
00:36:11.835 --> 00:36:16.639
Its intercept is log n plus m log ΔMeq.

483
00:36:16.679 --> 00:36:18.955
That is formula B.13.

484
00:36:18.995 --> 00:36:22.653
Now read that curve at 1 million cycles.

485
00:36:22.693 --> 00:36:30.711
The moment range there is ΔMo, formula
B.12: one over m, times log a bar minus six.

486
00:36:30.751 --> 00:36:34.341
If we do this for our histogram
with m equal to three,

487
00:36:34.441 --> 00:36:43.303
we get about 1335 kilonewton metres,
7 % below the 1436 in table A.7.

488
00:36:43.583 --> 00:36:47.800
So the tabulated single load
value is slightly conservative.

489
00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:51.218
A nice check that we
understand what it means.

490
00:36:52.183 --> 00:36:53.967
Here is the payoff.

491
00:36:54.007 --> 00:36:59.437
If the demand and the capacity are
both written at 1 million cycles,

492
00:36:59.607 --> 00:37:07.144
the damage of one year is just
ΔMo over ΔMc, to the power m.

493
00:37:07.184 --> 00:37:14.757
So the fatigue life is the histogram
period times ΔMc over ΔMo, to the m.

494
00:37:14.797 --> 00:37:20.437
Require that life divided by the
design factor is at least one year,

495
00:37:20.477 --> 00:37:26.913
take both sides to the power one over
m, and you get the screening check:

496
00:37:26.953 --> 00:37:34.363
ΔMc divided by D F to the one
over m must be at least ΔMo.

497
00:37:34.403 --> 00:37:41.863
In moment space, a design factor
of 10 is only a factor of 2.15,

498
00:37:42.003 --> 00:37:45.326
because the moment enters
to the power three.

499
00:37:45.606 --> 00:37:52.756
The flip side: 10 % more moment costs a
quarter of the life with m equal to three,

500
00:37:52.906 --> 00:37:56.848
and almost 40 % with m equal to five.

501
00:38:00.983 --> 00:38:03.421
Time to put it all together.

502
00:38:03.461 --> 00:38:10.181
Here is our example well: 350 metres
of water, BOP on the wellhead,

503
00:38:10.221 --> 00:38:14.001
a 36 inch conductor and
a 20 inch surface casing,

504
00:38:14.151 --> 00:38:19.431
a four slot template with 2 millimetres
of clearance in the slot, soft clay,

505
00:38:19.471 --> 00:38:22.081
and 5 metres of cement shortfall.

506
00:38:22.121 --> 00:38:27.370
All inputs are illustrative teaching
values, not data from a real project.

507
00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:30.399
The steps, though, are
exactly the standard's.

508
00:38:31.350 --> 00:38:36.166
Step one: 29 bins, each
normalised range from table

509
00:38:36.206 --> 00:38:40.708
A.8 multiplied by 1436 kilonewton metres.

510
00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:45.409
First, the quick screening
check for the girth weld.

511
00:38:45.449 --> 00:38:49.918
ΔMc is 2780.

512
00:38:49.958 --> 00:38:58.244
Divide by 10 to the power one third, and
we get 1290, which is less than 1436.

513
00:38:58.284 --> 00:38:59.829
The screening fails.

514
00:38:59.869 --> 00:39:07.244
It predicts a fatigue life of only 7.3
years, and less than one year of service.

515
00:39:07.284 --> 00:39:12.384
But the screening uses a single
slope of three for every cycle.

516
00:39:12.665 --> 00:39:19.316
Most of our cycles live on the flatter slope
of five, where they do far less damage.

517
00:39:19.356 --> 00:39:22.152
So let's do the real calculation.

518
00:39:23.117 --> 00:39:30.206
For each bin, read N from the bilinear
M-N curve and divide the cycles by it.

519
00:39:30.246 --> 00:39:32.631
Look where the damage comes from.

520
00:39:32.671 --> 00:39:37.579
Not from the 4 million tiny cycles,
and not from the one or two monsters,

521
00:39:37.619 --> 00:39:42.480
but from the middle: moment
ranges between about 1000

522
00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:46.989
and 2300 kilonewton metres
do most of the work.

523
00:39:47.029 --> 00:39:51.922
The damage adds up to 0.071 per year.

524
00:39:52.202 --> 00:39:55.179
So the fatigue life is 14 years.

525
00:39:55.219 --> 00:40:01.216
Divide by the design factor of 10, and
the fatigue service life is 1.4 years,

526
00:40:01.256 --> 00:40:06.261
or about 513 days with a riser connected.

527
00:40:06.301 --> 00:40:08.556
More than the one year minimum.

528
00:40:08.596 --> 00:40:10.479
The girth weld passes.

529
00:40:11.417 --> 00:40:16.227
Compare the three answers:
7.3 years from the screening,

530
00:40:16.267 --> 00:40:22.444
9 years from a full sum with a single
slope, and 14 years with the bilinear curve.

531
00:40:22.484 --> 00:40:25.522
Each step removes a layer of conservatism.

532
00:40:25.562 --> 00:40:30.890
Use the screening to sort designs,
and do the full sum when it matters.

533
00:40:31.850 --> 00:40:34.832
The template weld gets the same treatment.

534
00:40:34.872 --> 00:40:41.022
Its damage is 0.028 per year,
a fatigue life of 36 years,

535
00:40:41.062 --> 00:40:47.828
and with a design factor of 10, about
3.5 years of fatigue service life.

536
00:40:47.868 --> 00:40:49.388
It passes too.

537
00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:53.000
Now play with the slot clearance.

538
00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:58.673
Close the gap completely, and the
template grabs a big share of the moment.

539
00:40:58.713 --> 00:41:03.102
The girth weld's service life
jumps to more than 7 years,

540
00:41:03.142 --> 00:41:08.228
but the template weld drops
below half a year and fails.

541
00:41:08.268 --> 00:41:12.734
Open the gap to 4 millimetres,
and the opposite happens.

542
00:41:12.774 --> 00:41:15.654
Remove the template contact altogether,

543
00:41:15.764 --> 00:41:18.761
and the girth weld fails
at just under one year.

544
00:41:18.801 --> 00:41:21.203
Load share moves damage around.

545
00:41:21.483 --> 00:41:23.400
It never deletes it.

546
00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:30.252
That is why clause 8.1 asks for the load
share to satisfy fatigue for the wellhead

547
00:41:30.292 --> 00:41:34.613
system, and the template,
at the same time.

548
00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:41.183
Here is how the girth weld's service
life responds to the other inputs.

549
00:41:41.223 --> 00:41:48.183
Fabrication quality is huge:
an SCF of 1.2 instead of 1.1,

550
00:41:48.223 --> 00:41:50.346
and the weld no longer passes.

551
00:41:50.386 --> 00:41:55.971
Water depth is huge: in
the 80 to 250 metre band

552
00:41:56.011 --> 00:42:00.115
the same well gets only a third of a year.

553
00:42:00.155 --> 00:42:06.228
And running the BOP on a christmas
tree cuts the life by more than half.

554
00:42:06.268 --> 00:42:11.458
Soil stiffness and cement shortfall
matter less for this particular weld,

555
00:42:11.568 --> 00:42:14.985
though they matter a lot for
welds on the inner string.

556
00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:19.744
So what if your well is in
shallow water and fails?

557
00:42:19.784 --> 00:42:22.140
NORSOK points to several levers.

558
00:42:22.180 --> 00:42:27.343
The template can be prepared for
a lateral BOP support system.

559
00:42:27.383 --> 00:42:30.423
If it removes 40 % of the moment,

560
00:42:30.543 --> 00:42:36.565
our shallow water girth weld goes from
a third of a year to more than 2 years.

561
00:42:36.605 --> 00:42:44.305
Table J.1 lists operational levers: less
LMRP overpull, a softer lower flex joint,

562
00:42:44.345 --> 00:42:49.683
a better riser tally, lower drill
pipe tension, and avoiding beam seas.

563
00:42:49.723 --> 00:42:54.516
And monitoring the wellhead during
operations replaces the conservative design

564
00:42:54.556 --> 00:43:00.382
histogram with measured loads, so the
fatigue life can be updated, and extended.

565
00:43:04.517 --> 00:43:07.972
Let's recap the whole method on one page.

566
00:43:08.012 --> 00:43:11.262
Take the annual histogram
at the wellhead datum,

567
00:43:11.382 --> 00:43:14.394
and remember that the
moment grows below it.

568
00:43:14.434 --> 00:43:19.443
Follow the load paths: stiffness
attracts load, gaps make it nonlinear.

569
00:43:19.483 --> 00:43:23.082
Below the mudline the
conductor is a beam on springs,

570
00:43:23.122 --> 00:43:27.004
with a peak moment just below the seabed.

571
00:43:27.044 --> 00:43:29.865
Convert moments to hot
spot stresses with beam

572
00:43:29.905 --> 00:43:32.927
theory and stress concentration factors.

573
00:43:32.967 --> 00:43:37.211
Build lower bound M-N
curves from a local model.

574
00:43:37.251 --> 00:43:42.973
Add up the damage with Palmgren Miner,
divide the life by the design factor,

575
00:43:43.013 --> 00:43:48.540
and check it against at least
one year of riser connected time.

576
00:43:49.483 --> 00:43:55.372
A conductor and a template slot are among
the few parts of a subsea well that have

577
00:43:55.412 --> 00:44:00.923
to last for the whole life of the
field, and can never be inspected.

578
00:44:00.963 --> 00:44:06.323
The method in NORSOK
U-001 lets us put a number

579
00:44:06.363 --> 00:44:09.452
on how much riser time they can take.

580
00:44:09.492 --> 00:44:15.230
Use it with care, check the S-N data
against the current DNV edition,

581
00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:17.641
and keep monitoring.

582
00:44:17.681 --> 00:44:19.322
Thanks for watching.

